<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. Amundsen asked for a same-day visit for his right ankle. Yesterday evening, pulling a small boat up the shore below his house, he stepped on a wet rock and turned his foot inward. He felt pain on the outside of the ankle and it swelled within an hour, but he walked back up to the house, slowly, and has walked on it today with a limp. He did not hear a crack. He has no numbness or pain in the foot itself, and no pain in the calf or knee. He took acetaminophen last night, which helped. He takes duloxetine 60 mg daily and uses capsaicin cream on his fingers. A friend offered him tramadol tablets, which he has not taken. He asked whether he needs an X-ray. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Onset</td><td>Yesterday evening</td></tr><tr><td>Location</td><td>Outside of the right ankle</td></tr><tr><td>Duration</td><td>About 18 hours</td></tr><tr><td>Character</td><td>Aching, sharp on turning the foot</td></tr><tr><td>Severity</td><td>5 of 10 walking, 2 of 10 at rest</td></tr><tr><td>Timing</td><td>Constant, worse on walking</td></tr><tr><td>Context</td><td>Turned the foot inward on a wet rock; on duloxetine</td></tr><tr><td>Modifying factors</td><td>Better with rest, elevation and acetaminophen</td></tr><tr><td>Associated signs and symptoms</td><td>Swelling and bruising; walked on it since; no numbness</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

